lofibrosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative/Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.125, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP and normal differential slightly reduce the likelihood of lymphoproliferative/plasma cell disorders but do not definitively rule them out."]}}, "frontier": ["B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fever) with marked leukocytosis support myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts (B2)?", "summary": "Constitutional symptoms alone with normal differential are insufficient to support myeloid neoplasm with blasts; the absence of blast cells in the differential weakens this hypothesis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the combination of marked leukocytosis with normal differential and elevated LAP strongly support chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (B3)?", "summary": "The normal differential and elevated LAP are classic features of chronic myeloid disorders, providing moderate evidence in favor of B3 over acute myeloid processes."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the elevated leukocyte alkaline phosphatase disfavor reactive/non-malignant leukocytosis (B1) in favor of neoplastic processes?", "summary": "Elevated LAP directly disfavors reactive causes of leukocytosis, shifting evidence toward neoplastic explanations."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal leukocyte differential argue against myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts (B2) in favor of chronic myeloproliferative processes?", "summary": "The absence of blast cells in the differential weakens support for B2 and aligns more with chronic myeloproliferative patterns."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute time course over days argue against chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm 