 explain extreme WBC and elevated LAP", "Chronic myeloproliferative disorder (e.g., CML): elevated LAP suggests this mechanism over acute processes", "Stress-induced leukocytosis from anxiety: plausible but requires higher-tier evidence (no cortisol/steroid data)", "Visceral mass syndrome (e.g., splenic/liver pathology): low-tier due to lack of localized findings in this step"], "alarm_features": ["Unexplained weight loss", "Mild fever (37.5°C)", "Marked leukocytosis with elevated LAP", "Hypertension (150/100 mmHg)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Reactive/Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.176, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Elevated LAP directly disfavors reactive causes of leukocytosis, shifting evidence toward neoplastic explanations."]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.008, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The absence of blast cells in the differential weakens support for B2 and aligns more with chronic myeloproliferative patterns."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.36505081392211525, "posterior": 0.357839343342923, "danger": 0.6, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The normal differential and elevated LAP are classic features of chronic myeloid disorders, providing moderate evidence in favor of B3 over acute myeloid proce…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3", "B3.4"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential